Upgrade to PHP 8.5
******************

{{maintitle: Upgrade Your Code to PHP 8.5 Automatically}}
{{description: Upgrade to PHP 8.5: DressCode rewrites your code to the pipe operator, array_last() and clone() with properties, and fixes or reports all PHP 8.5 deprecated.}}

.[perex]
Raise the PHP version in `composer.json` to 8.5, and DressCode rewrites your code to the pipe operator, `array_first()`, `array_last()` and `clone()` with properties. It also fixes or reports everything PHP 8.5 deprecated, and formats the result by your coding standard in the same run.


What It Looks Like
==================

A class written for PHP 8.4, in a project that now requires PHP 8.5. The configuration adds the sets `modernizations` and `deprecations` to the standard and says that the namespaces of the project declare no functions of their own; the target PHP version, which DressCode otherwise takes from `composer.json`, is given here by the key `targets`:

```neon
use:
	- perCs
	- modernizations
	- deprecations

nameResolution: certain

targets:
	php: '8.5'
```

```php .[before]
final class Article
{
    private string $title = '';

    /** @var list<Item> */
    private array $items = [];

    public function withTitle(string $title): static
    {
        $clone = clone $this;
        $clone->title = $title;
        return $clone;
    }

    public function slug(string $title): string
    {
        return trim(strtolower(strip_tags($title)));
    }

    public function newest(): Item
    {
        return $this->items[array_key_last($this->items)];
    }

    public function download(string $url): string
    {
        $ch = curl_init($url);
        curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER, true);
        $data = (string) curl_exec($ch);
        curl_close($ch);
        return $data;
    }

    public function count(): int
    {
        return (integer) count($this->items);
    }
}
```

After one `dresscode fix`:

```php .[after]
final class Article
{
    private string $title = '';

    /** @var list<Item> */
    private array $items = [];

    public function withTitle(string $title): static
    {
        return clone($this, ['title' => $title]);
    }

    public function slug(string $title): string
    {
        return $title |> strip_tags(...) |> strtolower(...) |> trim(...);
    }

    public function newest(): Item
    {
        return array_last($this->items);
    }

    public function download(string $url): string
    {
        $ch = curl_init($url);
        curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER, true);
        $data = (string) curl_exec($ch);
        return $data;
    }

    public function count(): int
    {
        return (int) count($this->items);
    }
}
```

Each change is one item of PHP 8.5: `clone()` with properties, the pipe operator `|>`, the function `array_last()`, the call of `curl_close()` that no longer does anything, and the cast `(integer)`, which PHP 8.5 deprecated.


What Gets Rewritten
===================

The set `modernizations` rewrites code to what PHP 8.5 brought. Each item is a decision of the section [upgrading |decisions/upgrading] that a project can also turn on alone.

| feature | before | after | fix |
|---|---|---|---|
| pipe operator, `upgrading.syntax.pipe` | `trim(strtolower($s))` | `$s \|> strtolower(...) \|> trim(...)` | fixed |
| `array_first()`, `array_last()`, `upgrading.functions.arrayFirstLast` | `$a[array_key_last($a)]` | `array_last($a)` | fixed |
| `clone()` with properties, `upgrading.syntax.cloneWith` | `$c = clone $this; $c->title = $title;` | `clone($this, ['title' => $title])` | fixed; risky where evaluating an argument once may matter |
| `#[\Deprecated]` on a trait and a constant, `upgrading.phpdoc.deprecated` | `/** @deprecated */` | `#[\Deprecated]` | risky: the deprecated code starts reporting at runtime |
| `#[\Override]` on a property, `upgrading.classes.Override` | `public string $name;` | `#[\Override] public string $name;` | in no set, turn it on yourself; needs types |

.[note]
Every decision knows the PHP version that brought its feature. While `composer.json` still allows PHP 8.4, the rewrites to 8.5 wait quietly, and `dresscode config` lists them as having no effect.


What PHP 8.5 Deprecated
=======================

The set `deprecations` fixes what has a direct replacement and reports the rest, with the version that deprecated it. Methods of PHP classes are rewritten only where DressCode knows the type of the object, see [Types from PHPStan |types].

| deprecated | DressCode |
|---|---|
| `curl_close()`, `curl_share_close()`, `finfo_close()`, `imagedestroy()`, `xml_parser_free()` | fixed: the call is removed, it no longer does anything |
| backticks <code>''`git log`''</code> | fixed: `shell_exec("git log")` |
| casts `(integer)`, `(boolean)`, `(double)`, `(binary)` | fixed: `(int)`, `(bool)`, `(float)`, `(string)` |
| a semicolon after `case` | fixed: `case 1:` |
| the array key `null` | fixed: `''` |
| `SplObjectStorage::contains()`, `attach()`, `detach()` | fixed with types: `offsetExists()`, `offsetSet()`, `offsetUnset()` |
| `ReflectionProperty::setAccessible()` | fixed with types: the call is removed |
| `mysqli_execute()`, `socket_set_timeout()` | reported |
| `DATE_RFC7231`, `FILTER_DEFAULT`, `MHASH_*` | reported |
| `__sleep()`, `__wakeup()` | reported: a rewrite to `__serialize()` would change the format of stored data |

A finding names the version that deprecated the thing:

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Db.php:19:5: error: Function `mysqli_execute()` is deprecated: since PHP 8.5. [upgrading.php.deprecatedApi]
\--


Risky Fixes
===========

A rewrite that may change what the code does is only reported, and made once you list its decision in `fixRisky`. The attribute `#[\Deprecated]` is such a case by its nature, because the deprecated code starts reporting at runtime:

```neon
fixRisky:
	- upgrading.phpdoc.deprecated
```

.[warning]
In a namespace, an unqualified `strip_tags()` may be a function of that namespace. Until the configuration says `nameResolution: certain`, the rewrites of such calls wait as risky, see [Functions and Constants in Namespaces |namespaces].


Step by Step
============

1. Start with a clean working tree, so that the diff shows only what DressCode did.
2. Measure how much work the move takes: `dresscode upgrade estimate php 8.5`. If the project sets `config.platform.php`, raise it to 8.5 first.
3. Save the plan and carry it out: `dresscode upgrade plan php 8.5 --save`, then `dresscode upgrade run`. At the Composer step, DressCode asks whether it may raise `require.php` to `^8.5`; you can also raise it yourself and then run `upgrade run`.
4. Read what is left for you, and run `upgrade run` again until the run ends with `UPGRADED`. The risky fixes that wait are decided by the types, by `nameResolution`, or by you.
5. Commit the result on its own, without other changes.

If the project has the sets `modernizations` and `deprecations` in its configuration, a plain `dresscode fix` after raising `require.php` is enough. [Upgrade Your Code Step by Step |upgrade] describes the whole procedure with tests and commits.


